॰ ॥ RUNG 85 — PRE-SEED THE DIAGNOSTIC RECORD, AND SEE IF THE FAULT GOES ॥ (2026-09-18)
॰ Rung 84 native wrote 933 of the interpreted 974 octets and they are an EXACT
॰ PREFIX — 232 of 242 records byte-identical, then `BadAccess addr ०`. The
॰ compiled encoder is not computing wrong values; it computes correct ones and
॰ then reaches a null.
॰
॰ THE NEXT RECORD THE INTERPRETER WRITES IS `(१, ७७७७)`, which is the dump at
॰ encode.t1:6174 reading `अन्तिमसङ्केतनदोषः ॱ पङ्क्ति`. That global is declared
॰ `सङ्केतनदोष भवति ०` (encode.t1:1514), and the margin four lines above the seed
॰ says what that means: "a WORD until something constructs a record in it, and a
॰ field read before that faults". `A global declared ० is a word whatever its
॰ type` is a native-only shape this corpus already carries.
॰
॰ THIS IS A FALSIFIER, NOT A FIX. `सङ्केतनॱसङ्केतनदोषरचना` is called FIRST, so the
॰ global holds a real record before anything reads a field off it. Then:
॰   past record 232 → the unconstructed global WAS the fault, and constructing it
॰                      at startup is the repair
॰   same fault      → it is not, and the 7777 record is a coincidence of position
॰
॰ AND IT ALSO ANSWERS A QUESTION ABOUT MY OWN INSTRUMENT. Rung 83 faults with the
॰ dump OFF and rung 84 with it ON; Δimage 424 against Δpc 420 leaves 4 octets
॰ unaccounted, which is consistent with one site plus code added after it AND
॰ with a site one instruction earlier. Strict equality cannot separate those, so
॰ the earlier reading is not evidence either way. If seeding moves rung 85 past
॰ 232 records, the dump's field read was real and not an artefact of turning the
॰ dump on.
